for an in-depth look we're joined by  Kathleen Kingsbury she is the deputy  editorial page editor at The New York  Times Katie good morning hi good morning  let's start with a Korean summit which  that The Times called a carefully  choreographed diplomatic dance is there  the potential for real progress here or  there just a lot of optics at work yeah  I think everyone wants to be cautiously  optimistic at this point you know we  know that the North Koreans especially  are great at theater and choreography  and that's what we saw on Friday but we  really do want to hold out hope of a  nuclear-free Korea and we want to see  peace in that region and so I think you  know there's still a lot of information  to come but right now it's good to  celebrate this this development so what  what was president Trump hoping would  come out of this in particular as his  summit with kim jeong-hoon approaches in  the coming weeks yeah I think the White  House is definitely seized this is a  victory I think that however the North  Koreans have spent a lot of time  developing this weapons program the idea  that they're going to give it up  wholesale right away is probably not  going to happen but I do think that this  sets the groundwork for the president to  go and meet with them and to make some  serious demands I won't talk about Rear  Admiral Ronnie Jackson who withdrew his  name as a nominee for Secretary of  Veterans Affairs how significant is  setback do you think that is for the  White House I think it's actually a  major setback I think that one of the  things have gotten lost and the  questions around dr. Jackson's conduct  is the question of why was he ever  appointed to this position nominated to  this position to begin with the reality  is is that he had no qualifications  beyond being in the military and being a  doctor he had no management experience  and he was going to be running one of  the largest healthcare systems in the  country overseeing the care of veterans  and so I think that  is part of a pattern a troubling pattern  that we see from this white house that  doesn't seem to vet its candidates it  puts forward four large jobs and that  seems to often not really consider the  job that they're appointing that person  to before naming them and you know I  think it's interesting that we still  don't know who the next nominee is going  to be to run the VA it's a hugely  important organization and at this point  it's had no leadership for several  months now let's move on to that house  Intel committees report and it's  released 250 pages the Democrats are not  happy about it  they release our own Minority Report  what can you tell us about really what  came out of this well you know I I don't  know that there were any huge major  developments that came from this the  report was heavily redacted in fact  that's something that the Republicans  criticized it for you know we've found  some small new details about the  behavior of Michael Flynn for instance  but otherwise I think even especially  given the redacted state of it it became  clear that this report hadn't asked very  tough questions about the president's  conduct and his associates conduct I  think that where it just reinforces a  couple of things one is that it  concluded that there had been rushing  meddling in the election I mean that's a  major question what are we going to do  to make sure that that doesn't happen in  2018 and 2020 and then it also just  reinforces the importance I know the  president disagrees but it really  reinforces the importance of having  special counsel Moeller doing his  investigation and you know looking into  these really important questions about  interference by the Russians in the  election kathleen Kingsbury of the New  York Times thanks Katie thank you thank  you
 

